{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790994741,"nonce":"WNTQF7Mgz7OkHiVxS29D5uc79fs=","title":"A variant's colour is wrong while a scheme transition animates","message":"An element with `bg-primary hover:bg-primary-hover`, hovered, through a scheme switch driven by AnimationScheduler (setScheduler, then clock.tick, theme.tick, tree.flush each step): while the theme animates, the element's colour does not follow the hover token; it reads stale values that even move the wrong way (43 then 34 while primaryHover rises 139, 144), and is right only once the transition ends (148). Interaction.hovered reads true throughout.\n\nWith transition-colors added it ends on the base colour instead (primary 125, not primaryHover 148) while still hovered.\n\nThe variant is one computed, `hovered ? primaryHover.get() : base.get()`, reading Themed.brush computeds that depend on ThemeState.revision. It looks like the outer computed is evaluated against stale nested computeds when the revision changes, which points at nested computeds in blinc_abi's reactive bridge or in Blinc's graph.\n\nReproduce in tests/Smoke.hx: in the \"keeps up with a scheme transition\" section, use the class above, set Interaction.of(node).hovered to true and tick 60 steps first, then log the element's fill against schemeTheme.color(PrimaryHover) per step.","files":null}]}